onepicr","licence":"CC BY 2.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_of_the_Water_ski_facility_in_Bleibtreusee,_Germany_(47818538232).jpg","w":5464,"h":3070,"ok_print":true},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Hohe_Acht.JPG/960px-Hohe_Acht.JPG","thumb":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Hohe_Acht.JPG/500px-Hohe_Acht.JPG","caption":"Hohe Acht","author":"Jörg Braukmann","licence":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hohe_Acht.JPG","w":4000,"h":3000,"ok_print":true}],"intro":{"body":"The federal city of Bonn is one of the largest cities in the Cologne Lowland, second only to Cologne in population, with 330,000 residents in 2020. Despite the size, it maintains a cosy, relaxed atmosphere of a small town, featuring mostly low-rise buildings, a charming old town and a lot of greenery. Located only 28 km south of Cologne up the Rhine River, it has a wealth of museums and points of interest. Bonn was the capital of the pre-reunification Federal Republic of Germany (\"West Germany\") from 1949 through 1990. The city is still seat of many federal institutions, and remains a popular choice for large-scale exhibitions and conferences.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Bonn","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Bonn","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"facts":{"population":330579,"visit_h":16.8}},"highlights":[{"id":"hl-bonn-minster","name":"Bonn Minster","kind":"Cathedral","lat":50.73341,"lon":7.09979,"dist_km":0.3,"rank_score":7.7,"resolved_by":"catalogue","image":{"url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Bonner%20M%C3%BCnster.jpg?width=960","author":"Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de","licence":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonner_M%C3%